    "layer.hiragana.note": "Hiragana is the place to start. It writes the little words and endings that hold a sentence together, plus many everyday words. Modern Japanese runs left to right; novels and newspapers often run top to bottom instead.",
    "layer.katakana.note": "Katakana makes the same sounds as hiragana, in sharper shapes. It writes words borrowed from other languages (like コーヒー, coffee), foreign names, sound effects and words that need to stand out.",
    "layer.kanji.note": "Kanji are Chinese characters used in Japanese. Each stands for a meaning and usually has more than one reading, so you learn them inside the words they appear in. A sentence mixes kanji for the main ideas with hiragana for the grammar. This lesson shows a first handful, each with its most useful reading.",
